# You are one actor among several

Three obligations follow from a single fact: the environment, the verification and the result are shared with people and processes you cannot see. Nothing below names a tool, a container or a URL — those are values (the target repo's `AGENTS.md` → `## Configuration`) and project mechanics (this repo: `worktree-dev-workflow`).

## Never bounce shared state you do not own

Long-lived infrastructure that other work depends on — databases, gateways, service containers, another branch's running stack — is **not yours to restart**, however convenient. Start, stop, restart, recreate, or reset **only what you brought up yourself for the work at hand**.

This is a hazard of **availability**, and it is separate from the data hazard in `general-db-destructive-ops`. A plain, non-data-destroying stop is enough to cause real damage: stopping a database out from under a heavy operation leaves it with an unclean shutdown, and the crash recovery on restart can take hours and hold every dependent stack down with it. No data is lost, and the environment is still unusable.

The list of services nobody may bounce is a value (the target repo's `AGENTS.md` → key `Shared services`), as is the one command that starts your own (the target repo's `AGENTS.md` → key `Infra capabilities`). Anything broader than that one command — a stack-wide down/up, restarting the container runtime, recycling a volume, killing a service by name — needs approval.

**The exception has a fixed shape**, mirroring `general-db-destructive-ops`: describe exactly what you want to run and what it will disrupt, then wait for an explicit yes to **that specific action**. A general instruction — "implement the plan", "bring up the stack", "make it work" — is **not** approval.

```text
✅ "The shared database looks stuck in recovery. I'd like to restart it, which
    interrupts any in-flight query and briefly takes all dependent stacks
    offline. Shall I?"  → then wait.

❌ Silently bouncing the whole stack to get a clean start.
```

## Who verifies what

**Your** verification is the **automated test suite** plus reading the code. Run the relevant tests and report the result — that is the completeness bar for your turn.

**The human's** verification is the manual, in-browser check of the running feature. That is a deliberate handoff, not something you left undone:

- Do **not** open a browser, spawn a browser agent, or drive the UI yourself to "confirm it works". A browser is in scope **only** when the user explicitly asks for it.
- Never frame the handoff as a shortcoming on your side. "A manual browser check wasn't possible because no browser tooling was available" is **wrong** — no browser tooling is expected of you. Green automated tests plus a precise handoff is the correct, complete outcome.

## End the turn with where and what to test

Whoever picks this up next must not have to reconstruct where the change is running or what it changed. Close the turn with:

- **Where** — the address to open and the working directory it came from. Both are built from templates in `AGENTS.md` (keys `Test-URL template`, `Worktree root`, `Container / compose names`), never copied out of a rule or invented.
- **What** — two to four bullets naming exactly what to click and verify **for this change**. "Open the app and check it works" is not a checklist.

Name only what actually exists: if no stack was started, do not print a container line for one.

## Verification checklist

- [ ] Nothing shared was started, stopped, restarted or recreated — only what this work brought up itself.
- [ ] Any broader action was described with its consequences and explicitly approved for that specific action beforehand.
- [ ] Verification was the automated suite, and its result is reported.
- [ ] No browser was driven to self-confirm the feature, and the handoff is not framed as a blocker.
- [ ] The turn ends with where to test (from the `AGENTS.md` templates) and a change-specific what-to-test list.
